==English==
[[File:Rhizocarpon.jpg|thumb|right|Lichen growing on a rock.]]

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*leyǵʰ-}}
{{bor+|en|la|līchēn}}, from {{der|en|grc|λειχήν}}, from [[λείχω]] (''leíkhō'', “to lick”). Originally used of [[liverwort]]; the modern sense first recorded 1715.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|lī'kən}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈlaɪ.kən/}}<ref name="MWO">{{R:Merriam Webster Online}}</ref><ref name="Cam">{{R:Cambridge}}</ref><ref name="ODO">{{R:Lexico}}</ref>
* {{enPR|lĭ'chən}}, {{q|also, especially in the UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈlɪtʃ.ən/}}<ref name="MWO"/><ref name="Cam"/><ref name="ODO"/>
* {{audio|en|en-uk-lichen.ogg|a=UK}}
* {{rhymes|en|aɪkən|ɪtʃən|s=2}}
* {{homophones|en|liken|lycan}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~|s|lichen}}

# {{senseid|en|Q43142}}Any of many [[symbiotic]] [[organism]]s, being [[association]]s of [[alga]]e and [[fungus|fungi]], often found as white or yellow-to-blue–green patches on rocks, old walls, etc.
#* {{RQ:Kipling Jungle Book|chapter=Lukannon|page=122|passage=The Beaches of Lukannon–the winter wheat so tall, / The dripping, crinkled '''lichens''', and the sea-fog drenching all!}}
#* {{RQ:Wells Time Machine|XI||It was the same rich green that one sees on forest moss or on the '''lichen''' in caves: plants which like these grow in a perpetual twilight.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1915|author=w:John Muir|title=s:Travels in Alaska|chapter=V
|passage=The nibble marks of the stone adze were still visible, though crusted over with scale '''lichens''' in most places.}}
# {{lb|en|figurative}} Something which gradually spreads across something else, causing damage.
#: {{synonyms|en|cancer}}
#* {{RQ:Grey Riders of the Purple Sage|chapter=Shadows on the Sage-slope|page=202|passage=Meanwhile, abiding a day of judgment, she fought ceaselessly to deny the bitter drops in her cup, to tear back the slow, the intangibly slow growth of a hot, corrosive '''lichen''' eating into her heart.}}

====Hyponyms====
* {{sense|symbiotic organism}} {{l|en|macrolichen}}, {{l|en|microlichen}}

